WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.322
In the lingering darkness of the dawn, Passepartout had climbed up the funeral pyre from the back, where he couldn't be seen.

2
00:00:08.323 --> 00:00:14.753
Then, profiting from the billows of smoke, he had braved the flames and saved the woman from death!

3
00:00:14.754 --> 00:00:20.021
Without losing a moment, the four men disappeared into the jungle with Aouda.

4
00:00:20.022 --> 00:00:25.063
They returned to the elephant and, thanks to their skillful guide, escaped quickly.

5
00:00:25.064 --> 00:00:30.922
For the first hour, Passepartout laughed happily at his success.

6
00:00:30.923 --> 00:00:40.592
Sir Francis shook his hand heartily. Fogg said simply, "Well done!" which, from him, was high praise, indeed.

7
00:00:40.593 --> 00:00:48.697
Wrapped in a traveling blanket, Aouda rested on Kiouni's back until they reached the train station at Allahabad.

8
00:00:48.698 --> 00:00:53.633
It was ten o'clock, and the journey to Calcutta would take 24 hours.

9
00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:59.938
They would have just enough time to catch the steamer for Hong Kong on October 25 at noon.

10
00:00:59.939 --> 00:01:05.776
Passepartout was sent to purchase some necessities for Aouda, including a dress and a shawl.

11
00:01:05.777 --> 00:01:11.848
While he was away, Mr. Fogg and Sir Francis helped Aouda to a waiting room in the station.

12
00:01:11.849 --> 00:01:17.109
They watched her anxiously, waiting for her to revive from her long slumber.

13
00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:23.568
She had delicate cheeks, long silken lashes, fine white teeth, and smiling lips.

14
00:01:23.569 --> 00:01:27.333
The men could not help but notice how charming she was.

15
00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:31.764
Passepartout returned a little before the train was about to leave.

16
00:01:31.765 --> 00:01:36.580
Outside the station Mr. Fogg was paying the guide, who stood with Kiouni.

17
00:01:36.581 --> 00:01:39.595
"You have been a great help," he was saying.

18
00:01:39.596 --> 00:01:43.628
"I have paid you for your service but not for your devotion.

19
00:01:43.629 --> 00:01:47.920
Would you like to have this elephant? He is yours."

20
00:01:47.921 --> 00:01:54.619
The guide's eyes glistened. "You are giving me a fortune!" he cried.

21
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.426
Mr. Fogg and his friends then boarded the train.

22
00:01:58.427 --> 00:02:03.660
Kiouni trumpeted good-bye, and the guide waved as the train left the station.

23
00:02:03.661 --> 00:02:12.555
Phileas Fogg, Sir Francis Cromarty, Passepartout, and Aouda were soon seated in their car, traveling at full speed.

24
00:02:12.556 --> 00:02:20.828
It took two hours to reach the train's first stop at Benares, and during the journey, Aouda fully recovered from her drowsiness.

25
00:02:20.829 --> 00:02:28.275
She was astonished to find herself in a car on a train, dressed in European clothing, and surrounded by strangers.

26
00:02:28.276 --> 00:02:32.707
Her companions immediately told her what had transpired.

27
00:02:32.708 --> 00:02:38.616
With tears in her eyes, she thanked her saviors repeatedly.

28
00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:43.864
She knew, however, that her husband's family would not stop searching for her.

29
00:02:43.865 --> 00:02:48.027
Danger would follow her as long as she remained in India.

30
00:02:48.028 --> 00:02:55.146
When she explained that she had a merchant relative in Hong Kong, Mr. Fogg offered to escort her there safely.

31
00:02:55.147 --> 00:03:02.233
At half-past 12, the train arrived in Benares. This was Sir Francis' destination.

32
00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:06.488
He wished them all success on their journey and said good-bye.

33
00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:10.962
From Benares the train passed through the valley of the Ganges River. The

34
00:03:10.963 --> 00:03:19.128
travelers could see mountains; fields of barley, wheat, and corn; and jungles with green alligators. They

35
00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:26.206
passed villages at the edges of thick forests and elephants bathing in sacred rivers. Night

36
00:03:26.207 --> 00:03:30.248
fell and the train continued at full speed. In

37
00:03:30.249 --> 00:03:38.091
the dark the passengers heard the roars of tigers, bears, and wolves, which fled at the sound of the locomotive. The

38
00:03:38.092 --> 00:03:42.904
train reached Calcutta at seven in the morning on October 25. According

39
00:03:42.905 --> 00:03:48.321
to Mr. Fogg's journal, that was the exact date scheduled for his arrival. He

40
00:03:48.322 --> 00:03:52.341
was neither behind nor ahead of time. The

41
00:03:52.342 --> 00:03:57.679
two days he had gained between London and Bombay had been lost in the journey across India. But

42
00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.597
Phileas Fogg did not regret the loss in the least! The

43
00:04:01.598 --> 00:04:05.256
train entered the station, and Passepartout jumped out first. Mr.

44
00:04:05.257 --> 00:04:11.082
Fogg followed and held out his arm to help his fair companion down from the train. Mr.

45
00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:18.130
Fogg intended to proceed at once to the Hong Kong steamer so that Aouda could get comfortably settled for the voyage. He

46
00:04:18.131 --> 00:04:22.408
was unwilling to leave her side while she remained on dangerous ground. Just

47
00:04:22.409 --> 00:04:29.520
as they were leaving the station, a policeman approached Mr. Fogg and said, "Mr. Phileas Fogg?" "I

48
00:04:29.521 --> 00:04:31.905
am he," replied Fogg. "Is

49
00:04:31.906 --> 00:04:36.764
this your servant?" asked the policeman, pointing to Passepartout. "Yes,"

50
00:04:36.765 --> 00:04:39.355
said Mr. Fogg. "Both

51
00:04:39.356 --> 00:04:44.509
of you, be good enough to follow me," commanded the policeman. Mr.

52
00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:48.406
Fogg betrayed no surprise or alarm. Passepartout

53
00:04:48.407 --> 00:04:51.879
tried to explain that they had no minutes to spare, but

54
00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.480
the policeman tapped him with his stick, and Mr. Fogg gave Passepartout a signal to obey. "May

55
00:04:57.481 --> 00:05:01.393
this young lady come with us?" asked Mr. Fogg. "She

56
00:05:01.394 --> 00:05:04.788
may," replied the policeman. Mr.

57
00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:12.208
Fogg, Aouda, and Passepartout were conducted to a four-wheeled carriage drawn by two horses. They took

58
00:05:12.209 --> 00:05:17.046
their seats next to the policeman and were soon on their way. During the

59
00:05:17.047 --> 00:05:27.675
twenty-minutes that elapsed between leaving the train station and arriving at the courthouse, no one spoke. Passepartout's Guidebook

60
00:05:27.676 --> 00:05:34.569
As our train traveled to Calcutta, we saw the famous Ganges River. I must say, it was beautiful.

61
00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:40.124
The river runs a little more than 1,500 miles, from the Himalayas all the way to the Bay of Bengal,

62
00:05:40.125 --> 00:05:44.402
through some of the most fertile and populated land in the world.

63
00:05:44.403 --> 00:05:48.744
According to Hinduism, the Ganges River is the holiest of rivers.

64
00:05:48.745 --> 00:05:54.025
Its water is considered holy, and Hindus believe that bathing in it will wash away sin.

65
00:05:54.026 --> 00:05:59.986
As a result many Indians travel great distances every year to drink and bathe in the river's holy water.

66
00:05:59.987 --> 00:06:08.927
It is also believed that spreading one's ashes in the river after death leads to quicker salvation.

